# =============================================================================
# defense.manifest -- which defense this branch carries, and how to run it.
#
# THIS IS THE ONLY FILE THAT DIFFERS BETWEEN BRANCHES.
#
# tools/olsr-research.sh and tools/run_simulations.sh are byte-identical on
# every branch; they read this file to learn what to build and which flags a
# faithful run needs. That is what makes "switch branch, run dataset" a single
# command that cannot be configured wrongly by accident.
#
# It is sourced as a shell fragment, so: KEY=value, quote anything with spaces.
#
# FIELDS
#   DEFENSE_ID                short name; also the --defense selector and the
#                             prefix of generated dataset directory names.
#                             'none' means this branch carries no harness.
#   DEFENSE_NAME              human-readable name, shown in banners.
#   DEFENSE_BRANCH            the branch this manifest belongs to. 'doctor'
#                             compares it against HEAD, which catches a
#                             manifest that arrived on the wrong branch
#                             through a careless merge.
#   PAPER                     the publication being reproduced.
#   SCRATCH_TARGET            scratch program, without the .cc extension.
#                             The cmake target is scratch_<SCRATCH_TARGET>.
#   DEFENSE_FLAGS             harness arguments that EVERY faithful run needs.
#                             Applied automatically by olsr-research.sh.
#   BATCH_SEED_*              first --run seed of each canonical batch. The
#                             ranges are disjoint so batches stay statistically
#                             independent; keep them as published in
#                             docs/DATASETS.md or the existing datasets stop
#                             being comparable.
# =============================================================================

DEFENSE_ID=trust
DEFENSE_NAME="TRUST-OLSR"
DEFENSE_BRANCH=trust-defense
PAPER="Adnane, Bidan & de Sousa -- Trust-based security for the OLSR routing protocol, Computer Communications 36 (2013)"
SCRATCH_TARGET=olsr-trust-eval-mitigation

# WITHOUT THESE TWO FLAGS THE DEFENSE IS MOSTLY SWITCHED OFF.
#
# The harness CLI defaults are enableConsistencyRules=0 and
# enableAlertDistribution=0, so only Formula 10 (the forward monitor) is live.
# Measured detection with the defaults was 0.12% static / 0.48% mobile, which
# reads like the defense does not work. With these flags:
#
#   --enableConsistencyRules=1   Formulas 6, 7, 8, 9a, 9b, 11/12 and the
#                                extended network-wide rule of section 5.1
#   --enableAlertDistribution=1  section 7 trust-alert re-broadcast
#
# Provable identity (section 6) stays off deliberately: the harness hard-sets
# EnableProvableIdentity=false, which matches the measured recommendation --
# at this scale it costs more than it gives.
#
# See docs/RUNNING.md, "The TRUST default-flags trap".
DEFENSE_FLAGS="--enableConsistencyRules=1 --enableAlertDistribution=1"

# As published in docs/DATASETS.md (the trust_static_v2 / trust_mobile_v2
# batches). The v2 batches deliberately reuse the v1 seed ranges so that the
# defense-off windows come out bit-identical and the two are comparable
# window-for-window.
BATCH_SEED_STATIC=4000001
BATCH_SEED_MOBILE=6000001
BATCH_SEED_STATIC_MIXED=14000001
BATCH_SEED_MOBILE_MIXED=16000001
